1. Accept three of:
- the enclosed space of the castle: *"the east tower, where no one had climbed for forty years"*;
- the **portrait**: the letter is hidden *"behind the portrait"*;
- the **found manuscript**: *"I found the letter"*, a text whose author and origin cannot be checked;
- the **family curse**: *"will share my fate, as my father shared his father's"*.
2. The narrator is **unreliable**: *"I laughed, I think"*, *"I cannot swear to what I saw next"*, *"My physician says I was feverish"*. He is not sure of his own memories, and another character suggests he was ill, so the reader cannot know whether the events are real.
3. *"Whoever reads this will share my fate"* is foreshadowing: it announces that the narrator, who has just read the letter, will suffer the same curse. The comparison *"like dried blood"* also hints at violence to come.
4. The text creates **dread**: we expect something terrible to happen. The footsteps in *"a room that had no floor"* are **uncanny**: an ordinary sound becomes impossible and disturbing. Because the narrator is unreliable, the fear stays an **effect**: we never know whether the supernatural is real.

